<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Histiaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 493 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Histiaeus was the tyrant of Miletus under the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in the late 6th century BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> he guarded Darius's bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dariuss-scythian-campaign">Scythian campaign</a> and was later kept at the Persian court, from where he encouraged his deputy and son-in-law Aristagoras to start the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> in 499 BCE. He was captured and executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in 493 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/histiaeus.jpg" alt="Histiaeus" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IONIA,_Miletos._Circa_600-550_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Histiaeus, the son of Lysagoras, was a Greek ruler of Miletus in the late 6th century BCE. He was tyrant of the city under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, who had subjugated Miletus and the other Ionian states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and generally appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as tyrants to rule the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Histiaeus took part in the expedition of Darius against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, around 513 BCE, and was put in charge of defending the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The Scythians urged the tyrants to abandon it, and a faction led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, then tyrant of the Chersonese, wished to follow their advice. Histiaeus argued that they owed their positions to Darius and would be overthrown if he were killed. He suggested that they pretend to accept the plan, and went as an ambassador to the Scythians to say so, while the others acted as if they were demolishing the bridge. When the Scythians went off to look for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, Histiaeus organised the ships that ferried the Persians across.</p><p>During the campaign his troops had begun to build a settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrcinus">Myrcinus</a> on the Strymon River, and Darius granted it to him as a reward. The Persian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabazus">Megabazus</a> suspected him of an interest in a place that controlled roads into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, but Darius thought him loyal and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> as a friend and adviser. His nephew and son-in-law Aristagoras was left in control of Miletus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> writes that Histiaeus, unhappy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, planned to return by instigating a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. In 499 BCE he shaved the head of his most trusted slave, tattooed a message on it, and sent the slave to Aristagoras once his hair had grown back, telling him to revolt. Aristagoras, disliked at home after a failed expedition to Naxos, obeyed and, with Athenian and Eretrian help, attacked and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. Darius let Histiaeus go to put the revolt down, but the satrap Artaphernes suspected him and he fled to Chios. When he returned to Miletus to become tyrant again, its people, who did not want tyranny back, exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, and he turned to piracy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> defeated the leaders of the revolt at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE. Histiaeus then attacked Chios and blockaded Thasos, and after joining a Greek force in battle against the Persians he was captured by the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> in 493 BCE. Artaphernes, fearing that Darius would pardon him, executed him by impaling and sent his head to Darius, who, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, did not believe that Histiaeus had been a traitor and gave the head an honourable burial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Histiaeus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 499 BCE Aristagoras, who governed the city for the tyrant <b>Histiaeus</b>, failed in a Persian-backed attack on Naxos and then led the Ionians in rebellion; with Athenian and Eretrian help the rebels burned Sardis in 498 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Miletus, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.53,27.28&amp;year=-503" aria-label="Miletus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5232 813 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Histiaeus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5682 1083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5682 1083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.53,27.28&amp;year=-503">What was here in 503 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
